  "hints": [
    "Removing two different values cannot eliminate an existing strict majority.",
    "Maintain a candidate and a vote balance, resetting the candidate when the balance reaches zero.",
    "Equal values add a vote; different values subtract one. Replace an exhausted candidate on the next element.",
    "Because a majority is guaranteed, the final surviving candidate needs no additional existence check."
  ],
  "editorial": "Boyer–Moore voting pairs each occurrence of a candidate with an opposing value whenever possible. A strict majority cannot be canceled entirely by all other values, so it is the final surviving candidate. Track only the candidate and its balance. If the existence guarantee were removed, a second pass would be required to verify the candidate.",
